[[:db/retract 17592186301242 :question/answer-fields 17592186301243] {:db/id "q-332547/mathematical-correction-v1/field-1/answer" :answer/id #uuid "709ba230-de60-5640-a3b1-3fac092014b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{-6x/5}-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332547/mathematical-correction-v1/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "52bc54ab-ed20-5e27-b5c2-8e5ab134ae4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["q-332547/mathematical-correction-v1/field-1/answer"] :answer-field/correct "q-332547/mathematical-correction-v1/field-1/answer" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186301242 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-30.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x/5},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x/5}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 0+6\\beta =-30\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x/5}-5.\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["q-332547/mathematical-correction-v1/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
